  "scientific_question": "Do CA1 reactive astrocyte hotspots sit in oligodendrocyte-poor niches, relative to matched homeostatic astrocyte hotspots, and does that focal glia-myelin support failure add signal beyond the current CA1 neuron-loss/gliosis panel?",
  "approach": "Assign cells to CA1 using CustomAnnotations polygons after dividing polygon coordinates by 16. From ClassificationNode labels, collect CA1 centroids for Reactive Astrocyte, Astrocyte, and Oligodendrocyte. Using a 70 px radius, define reactive hotspot centers as reactive astrocytes having at least k same-type neighbors within 70 px (excluding self), and define baseline astrocyte hotspot centers analogously among Astrocytes. For each hotspot center, count CA1 oligodendrocyte neighbors within 70 px. The donor biomarker is the median oligodendrocyte neighbor count around reactive hotspot centers minus the median count around astrocyte hotspot centers; more negative values indicate oligodendrocyte depletion around reactive hotspots.",
  "variations": [
    {
      "name": "candidate_variant_a",
      "description": "Reactive-versus-astrocyte hotspot oligodendrocyte depletion in CA1 with hotspot threshold k=3 same-type neighbors within 70 px."
    },
    {
      "name": "candidate_variant_b",
      "description": "Same biomarker but with a slightly stricter hotspot threshold k=4 same-type neighbors within 70 px."
    }
  ],
  "baseline_variation": "candidate_variant_a",
  "notes": "Use centroids only; no WSI pixels are required. Exclude the center cell from same-type neighbor counts. Restrict oligodendrocyte neighbor counting to CA1-assigned cells. Build KD-trees or equivalent for efficient radius queries. If one hotspot set is empty but the corresponding CA1 cell class exists, fall back to using all CA1 cells of that astroglial class as centers for that side so donor coverage stays high; return NaN only if CA1 or required cell classes are genuinely unavailable.",
  "rationale": "Recent rounds consistently improved by making the CA1 reactive astrocyte signal more focal: bulk enrichment, morphology, microclustering, hotspot exposure, and then hotspot-local pyramidal depletion. The next clean one-step extension is to keep the same CA1 hotspot framework but swap the neighboring readout from pyramidal neurons to oligodendrocytes, testing whether the accepted panel is missing a distinct myelin-support injury component rather than re-aggregating the same astrocyte or neuronal burden signal."
}